			</ul>
		</article>
	</section>
{% endblock %}
{% block listpane_help %}
	<header>
		<h1>The ticket status field tracks who needs to act on the ticket next.</h1>
		<p>
			Deskpro is based on back and forth communication between users and agents. The status field is used to record whether a user or an agent needs to respond to the ticket next. You cannot change or add statuses.
		</p>
	</header>
	<article>
		<section>
			<p>There are three core ticket statuses:</p>

				<ul>
					<li><strong>Awaiting Agent</strong>: The ticket requires a response from an agent. Deskpro is based around helping agents work on and respond to these tickets.</li>

					<li><strong>Awaiting User</strong>: The ticket requires a response from a user. Typically this will be because the Agent has replied and is either awaiting further information or has fixed the problem and is awaiting confirmation that the problem is fixed.</li>

					<li><strong>Resolved</strong>: The ticket is completed and does not require any further response.</li>

				</ul>
				<p>Click on each status below to see the number of tickets in your helpdesk with that status.</p>

			<p>Agents can set tickets to a sub-status called <strong>On Hold</strong>. This status only applies to tickets that are Awaiting Agent. This status is designed for tickets that agents cannot reply to currently (e.g. because they are waiting for a third party). Tickets on hold are accessible from the Awaiting Agent section of the agent interface filter pane.

			<p>Tickets can also have their status set to <strong>Spam</strong> or <strong>Deleted</strong>. You can set how long spam or deleted tickets are kept before they are automatically deleted by clicking on the relevant status.</p>
			<p>You can turn on <strong>archiving</strong> of old, resolved tickets. For very large helpdesks with millions of tickets, archiving can greatly improve performance. Click <strong>Archived</strong> to enable this, and specify how long before resolved tickets are archived.</p>
			<p>When an old ticket is archived it is given archived status. Agents can still view archived tickets from the Tickets app filter pane, and agents with the correct permission can restore archived tickets.</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2><span>FAQ</span></h2>
			<dl>
				<dt><em>Q:</em> Can I have a custom status?</dt>
				<dd><em>A:</em>
					No. Being able to add extra statuses would break the core flow of how Deskpro handles tickets.
					If you wish to track the state of a ticket more closely, we recommend you use the <strong>Workflow</strong> custom field.
				</dd>

				<dt><em>Q:</em> My helpdesk is slow; should I enable archiving?</dt>
				<dd><em>A:</em> Deskpro can handle the ticket volume of the vast majority of helpdesks.
					Archiving is only likely to improve performance if you have over a million resolved tickets.
					If you have fewer than that and your helpdesk is slow, you should check your internet connectivity
					(for Cloud customers) or the server configuration of your Deskpro installation
					(for Download customers).
				</dd>
			</dl>
		</section>
		<section class="related-content">
			<h2><span>Related Content</span></h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
				<i class="fa fa-book"></i> <a href="https://support.deskpro.com/guides/topic/278" target="_blank">Ticket status</a>
				</li>
				<li>
				<i class="fa fa-file-text"></i> <a href="https://support.deskpro.com/kb/articles/195" target="_blank">Why can't I add a custom ticket status?</a>
				</li>

			</ul>
		</section>
</article>
{% endblock %}
